Lucciola: start the login conversation when a user is selected

The greeter calls greetd's create_session only when the password is
submitted, so with a fingerprint reader in the PAM stack (Fedora's
authselect with-fingerprint: pam_fprintd sufficient, then pam_unix)
the reader starts listening only after Enter. A new key,
[auth].start_on_select (default false), opens the conversation as soon
as a user is selected and nothing is running: at startup, after a user
switch, after a failed attempt, after a logout (a new greeter). The
fingerprint prompt shows on the status line and a touch logs in.

With the key on:
- PAM's password prompt (no reader, no enrolled finger, pam_fprintd
  timed out) is answered as before: type, Enter. An empty Enter there
  starts a new conversation, so the reader listens again.
- A password submitted while pam_fprintd still waits is kept and sent
  at the password prompt that follows. greetd reads one request at a
  time per client and holds its lock while PAM waits, so neither an
  answer nor a cancel can reach PAM sooner; the field stays usable
  while PAM waits, and the status line says the password is ready.
- A user switch cancels the conversation (cancel_session; after the
  in-flight reply when there is one, whatever that reply says) and the
  next one starts for the new user.
- After a submitted attempt fails, the next conversation starts by
  itself and the failure stays on the status line next to its first
  message. A conversation that fails with no input from the user is not
  restarted until a submit, so it cannot fail in a loop.
- With several outputs there is one conversation: it moves to the
  output in use (the connection to greetd is shared) and survives an
  unplugged output; its messages show on every output.
With the key off nothing changes.

Applied by lucciola/greeter/noctalia-greeter.spec to the v1.6.0 tag,
after 0001. Could be proposed upstream (noctalia-dev/noctalia-greeter):
the key, its parsing and writing, the docs and the example are in the
patch.

